      "path": "src/modules/market-data/market-data.ts",
      "content": "When the range includes historical sessions and the session's own date, awaiting `scanOf` before calling `framesAt` forces these reads into separate batches and therefore separate IndexedDB snapshots. A save committed between them can make a single ReadSeries response combine historical points from before the save with the final point from after it. Queue both reads before awaiting either (for example, with `Promise.all`) so an uncached series reads its historical and final points in the same snapshot, consistent with the read-session batching contract.",
      "existing_code": "          const scanned = first === undefined ? [] : (await scanOf(instrumentKey, first.close)).points.filter((p) =\u003e p.tradingDate \u003e= from \u0026\u0026 p.tradingDate \u003c= end);\n          if (end \u003c day.date) return scanned;\n          const [frame] = await framesAt(context, day, [instrumentKey]);",
      "start_line": 942,
      "end_line": 944,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "src/modules/market-data/market-data.ts",
      "content": "A valid series request at the first covered session can throw here. `resolveDay` accepts that session, but `frameResult` rejects it when `previousSession(day.date)` is outside coverage, so `framesAt` returns an empty array and `frame!.resolution` dereferences undefined. A series point does not need the previous session: resolve the current date's observation directly, or propagate the coverage rejection explicitly instead of assuming a frame exists. Add a test with the context on the first covered session and a range including that date.",
      "existing_code": "          const [frame] = await framesAt(context, day, [instrumentKey]);\n          return [...scanned, seriesPoint(day.date, day.close, frame!.resolution)];",
